### Important audio availability requirement

Song metadata and audio playback are two separate things.

Do not pretend that a song is playable merely because its title and artist appear in the library.

Do not use fabricated audio files, silent placeholder tracks presented as real recordings, or random audio URLs.

Use a legitimate source for audio playback.

Preferred implementation options:

1. Integrate a supported music service or authorised playback provider where feasible.
2. Support locally supplied audio files through an upload control.
3. Support direct audio URLs where the user has permission to use them.
4. Provide a clearly labelled preview or external-listening option where a full recording cannot be played directly.

If an external music provider requires authentication, API credentials, subscription access, or user consent, explain the requirement and implement the appropriate connection flow instead of pretending it already works.

Do not assume that a public music API automatically grants access to full commercial recordings.

If no audio source is available for a catalogue entry, it may still appear as a searchable track, but it must be marked unavailable for in-app playback until a legitimate source is connected.

Where useful, include an optional local-file upload area so users can supply their own music.

The interface must make the difference between selecting a song, loading an audio source, and actually playing audio clear.

